{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III (c. 1145 – 31 October 1187) was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. He was the son of Count Raymond II and his wife, Constance of Antioch. He ascended to the throne at a young age, under the regency of his mother until 1163, when he assumed full control of the county. His reign was marked by significant challenges, including the rise of Nur ad-Din Zangi in Syria and the Crusader's unsuccessful efforts to maintain their foothold in the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III's rule was characterized by his efforts to strengthen the Crusader states through diplomatic and military means. He forged alliances with various local leaders, including Humphrey II of Toron and the Assassins, to counter the growing threat of the Ayyubids. He also faced internal conflicts, such as the revolt of his nephew, Bohemond III of Antioch, which he managed to suppress. His reign coincided with the Third Crusade, during which he played a critical role in the defense of the Crusader states against Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died on 31 October 1187, during the Battle of Hattin, one of the most decisive defeats for the Crusaders. His death marked a significant turning point in the history of the Crusades, as Saladin's victory led to the loss of most of the Crusader states in the Levant. Despite the challenges he faced, Raymond III's reign was a testament to the complex and often tumultuous nature of the Crusader states, and his efforts to maintain their existence in a hostile environment. His legacy is a reflection of the broader struggle for control in the region during the late 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -1.2285481691360474}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.9176306128501892}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": -0.0001003691868390888}, {"token"